# Conditional requests and entity tags (LWS 1.0 core section 9.3-9.5, RFC 9110 section 13).
"@context":
  - ../context.jsonld
  - ../touchstone.jsonld
id: "#manifest"
type: Manifest
label: "LWS 1.0 core: conditional requests and entity tags"
comment: >-
  The 21 September 2026 draft made support for conditional requests a SHOULD and dropped
  both "ETags MUST be provided in all GET/HEAD responses" from that paragraph and the
  requirement to answer an unconditional PUT with 428. What stays MUST here stays because
  RFC 9110 makes it so for any server that does evaluate a precondition: a 304 only when
  the validator matches, no write when If-Match fails, and a strong validator that
  changes whenever the representation does.
specification:
  - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20261005/
entries:

  - id: "#conditional-get-304"
    type: ValidationTest
    name: conditional-get-304
    label: An unchanged data resource answers a conditional GET with 304
    comment: >-
      Lets a client revalidate without re-transferring the content.
    status: Proposed
    level: SHOULD
    source:
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20261005/#read-resource
      - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-13.1.2
    traits: [Get, DataResource, Conditional]
    requirements:
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/conditional-requests-supported
    supersedes: [core/conditional-get-304]
    prereqs:
      hierarchy:
        - dataResource: created
          contentType: text/plain
          body: conditional request check
    steps:
      - label: read it and keep the validator
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: ETag
              present: true
              capture: etag
      - label: revalidating with that validator yields 304 and no body
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: If-None-Match
              headerValue: "${etag}"
        response:
          statusCode: 304
          bodyEmpty: true

  - id: "#conditional-get-container-304"
    type: ValidationTest
    name: conditional-get-container-304
    label: An unchanged container listing answers a conditional GET with 304
    comment: >-
      The draft names the container listing alongside the resource: "If the resource or
      container listing has not changed, respond with 304 Not Modified".
    status: Proposed
    level: SHOULD
    source:
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20261005/#read-resource
    traits: [Get, Container, Conditional]
    supersedes: [core/etag-on-head-and-container-listing]
    note: >-
      Same catalog drift as conditional-get-304.
    steps:
      - label: read the listing and keep its validator
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${test.container}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: ETag
              present: true
              capture: etag
      - label: revalidating the listing yields 304
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${test.container}"
          accept: application/lws+json
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: If-None-Match
              headerValue: "${etag}"
        response:
          statusCode: 304
          bodyEmpty: true

  - id: "#conditional-get-stale-validator-200"
    type: ValidationTest
    name: conditional-get-stale-validator-200
    label: A conditional GET yields 304 only when the validator still matches
    comment: >-
      The negative half of conditional GET. A server may decline to support conditional
      requests and always answer 200, which passes; what it may not do is answer 304 to
      a validator that does not match, which would starve clients of every update. RFC
      9110 makes the evaluation a MUST for any server that performs it.
    status: Proposed
    level: MUST
    source:
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20261005/#read-resource
      - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-13.1.2
      - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-13.2.2
    traits: [Get, Put, DataResource, Conditional]
    supersedes: [core/conditional-get-stale-validator-200]
    prereqs:
      hierarchy:
        - dataResource: created
          contentType: text/plain
          body: first revision
    steps:
      - label: read it and keep the validator
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: ETag
              present: true
              capture: etag
      - label: a validator that never matched anything is not a 304
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: If-None-Match
              headerValue: '"touchstone-never-a-real-etag"'
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          bodyMatches: first revision
      - label: change the resource, so the held validator goes stale
        request:
          method: PUT
          url: "${created}"
          contentType: text/plain
          body: second revision
          ifMatch: "${etag}"
        response:
          statusCode: [200, 204]
      - label: the stale validator yields the new representation, not a 304
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: If-None-Match
              headerValue: "${etag}"
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          bodyMatches: second revision

  - id: "#conditional-put-stale-if-match-412"
    type: NegativeTest
    name: conditional-put-stale-if-match-412
    label: A PUT carrying a stale entity tag is refused with 412 and changes nothing
    comment: >-
      Optimistic concurrency. The update operation "is rejected if the resource has been
      modified since it was last read", the HTTP binding answers 412, and RFC 9110 forbids
      performing the method when If-Match fails. The final read is the real assertion: a
      412 that wrote anyway is worse than no check at all.
    status: Proposed
    level: MUST
    source:
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20261005/#update-resource
      - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-13.1.1
    traits: [Put, DataResource, Conditional]
    supersedes: [core/conditional-if-match-mismatch-412]
    note: >-
      The touchstone manifest cited put-unconditional-rejected-428, a clause the 21
      September draft removed; it also accepted 409, which the current text no longer
      mentions.
    prereqs:
      hierarchy:
        - dataResource: created
          contentType: text/plain
          body: original content
    steps:
      - label: read it and keep the validator that is about to go stale
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: ETag
              present: true
              capture: stale
      - label: a first conditional write succeeds
        request:
          method: PUT
          url: "${created}"
          contentType: text/plain
          body: second content
          ifMatch: "${stale}"
        response:
          statusCode: [200, 204]
      - label: the same validator, now stale, is refused
        request:
          method: PUT
          url: "${created}"
          contentType: text/plain
          body: third content, must not land
          ifMatch: "${stale}"
        response:
          statusCode: 412
      - label: the refused write left the resource untouched
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          bodyMatches: '^second content$'


  - id: "#conditional-delete-stale-if-match-412"
    type: NegativeTest
    name: conditional-delete-stale-if-match-412
    label: A DELETE carrying a stale entity tag is refused with 412 and deletes nothing
    comment: >-
      "Clients MAY include an If-Match header with an ETag for concurrency checks", and
      servers SHOULD support conditional requests (RFC 9110). When they do, a DELETE whose
      If-Match no longer matches is refused with 412 and the resource survives: RFC 9110
      forbids performing the method when the precondition fails.
    status: Proposed
    level: SHOULD
    source:
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20261005/#delete-resource
      - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-13.1.1
    traits: [Delete, Put, DataResource, Conditional]
    requirements:
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/delete-if-match-optional
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/delete-success-204-conditional
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/conditional-requests-supported
    prereqs:
      hierarchy:
        - dataResource: created
          contentType: text/plain
          body: original content
    steps:
      - label: read it and keep the validator that is about to go stale
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: ETag
              present: true
              capture: stale
      - label: change it, so the validator goes stale
        request:
          method: PUT
          url: "${created}"
          contentType: text/plain
          body: changed content
          ifMatch: "${stale}"
        response:
          statusCode: [200, 204]
      - label: a DELETE with the stale validator is refused
        request:
          method: DELETE
          url: "${created}"
          ifMatch: "${stale}"
        response:
          statusCode: 412
      - label: the refused DELETE left the resource in place
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          bodyMatches: '^changed content$'

  - id: "#update-changes-strong-etag"
    type: ValidationTest
    name: update-changes-strong-etag
    label: A strong entity tag changes when the content changes
    comment: >-
      RFC 9110 defines a strong validator as one that changes whenever the representation
      data does, and the draft requires ETags "as defined in [RFC9110]". A server using
      weak tags is not bound by this, so a weak tag makes the test inapplicable.
    status: Proposed
    level: MUST
    source:
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20261005/#read-resource
      - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-8.8.1
    traits: [Get, Put, DataResource, Conditional]
    supersedes: [core/put-replace-with-if-match]
    prereqs:
      hierarchy:
        - dataResource: created
          contentType: text/plain
          body: version one
    steps:
      - label: its entity tag is strong (otherwise this test does not apply)
        precondition: true
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: ETag
              matches: '^"'
              capture: before
      - label: replace the content
        request:
          method: PUT
          url: "${created}"
          contentType: text/plain
          body: version two
          ifMatch: "${before}"
        response:
          statusCode: [200, 204]
      - label: the entity tag is a different one
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: ETag
              present: true
              differsFrom: "${before}"

  - id: "#delete-updates-parent-strong-etag"
    type: ValidationTest
    name: delete-updates-parent-strong-etag
    label: A container's strong entity tag changes when a member is deleted
    comment: >-
      The listing's representation changes when a member leaves, so a strong tag must
      change with it; a surviving tag tells every cache holding it that a stale listing
      is current. The 21 September draft dropped the explicit "its ETag MUST be updated"
      from the deletion paragraph, so this now rests on RFC 9110 and applies only to
      strong tags.
    status: Proposed
    level: MUST
    source:
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20261005/#delete-resource
      - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-8.8.1
    traits: [Get, Delete, Container, Conditional]
    requirements:
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/containment-delete-atomic-items
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/delete-removes-from-parent-listing
    supersedes: [core/delete-updates-parent-etag]
    note: >-
      Only the removal from the listing is a catalogued requirement: the tag check rests on
      RFC 9110, since the catalog entry for this paragraph no longer carries the ETag clause.
    prereqs:
      hierarchy:
        - dataResource: created
          contentType: text/plain
          body: not long for this world
    steps:
      - label: the listing's entity tag is strong (otherwise this test does not apply)
        precondition: true
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${test.container}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: ETag
              matches: '^"'
              capture: before
      - label: delete the member
        request:
          method: DELETE
          url: "${created}"
          ifMatch: current
        response:
          statusCode: 204
      - label: the listing has a new tag and no longer holds the member
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${test.container}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: ETag
              present: true
              differsFrom: "${before}"
          json:
            - pointer: /items
              none:
                - pointer: /id
                  equalsIri: "${created}"

  - id: "#conditional-get-if-modified-since-304"
    type: ValidationTest
    name: conditional-get-if-modified-since-304
    label: An unchanged data resource answers If-Modified-Since with 304, a changed one with 200
    comment: >-
      The draft names date-based validators alongside entity tags: "including mechanisms such
      as entity tags (ETags) and date-based validators (like If-Modified-Since headers)". A
      validator equal to the resource's Last-Modified means nothing changed since, so 304 and
      no body; one at the Unix epoch is older than any resource, so the representation itself.
      No If-None-Match is sent, which RFC 9110 would let take precedence.
    status: Proposed
    level: SHOULD
    source:
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20261005/#read-resource
      - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-13.1.3
    traits: [Get, DataResource, Conditional]
    requirements:
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/conditional-requests-supported
    note: >-
      From langsamu/LWS.net (Test/ConditionalRequestTests.cs, IfModifiedSince), rewritten to
      need no clock arithmetic: the epoch stands for "before", the server's own Last-Modified
      for "at".
    prereqs:
      hierarchy:
        - dataResource: created
          contentType: text/plain
          body: date validator check
    steps:
      - label: read it and keep its Last-Modified
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: Last-Modified
              present: true
              capture: lastModified
      - label: not modified since that date, so 304 and no body
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: If-Modified-Since
              headerValue: "${lastModified}"
        response:
          statusCode: 304
          bodyEmpty: true
      - label: modified since the epoch, so the representation
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: If-Modified-Since
              headerValue: "Thu, 01 Jan 1970 00:00:00 GMT"
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          bodyMatches: 'date validator check'

  - id: "#conditional-get-if-unmodified-since-412"
    type: NegativeTest
    name: conditional-get-if-unmodified-since-412
    label: If-Unmodified-Since passes at the resource's Last-Modified and fails with 412 before it
    comment: >-
      The other date-based validator of RFC 9110. At the resource's own Last-Modified the
      condition holds and the request proceeds; at the Unix epoch the resource has changed
      since, so the server answers 412. No If-Match is sent, which would take precedence.
    status: Proposed
    level: SHOULD
    source:
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20261005/#read-resource
      - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-13.1.4
    traits: [Get, DataResource, Conditional]
    requirements:
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/conditional-requests-supported
    note: >-
      From langsamu/LWS.net (Test/ConditionalRequestTests.cs, IfUnmodifiedSince), rewritten as
      conditional-get-if-modified-since-304 is.
    prereqs:
      hierarchy:
        - dataResource: created
          contentType: text/plain
          body: date validator check
    steps:
      - label: read it and keep its Last-Modified
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: Last-Modified
              present: true
              capture: lastModified
      - label: unmodified since that date, so the request proceeds
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: If-Unmodified-Since
              headerValue: "${lastModified}"
        response:
          statusCode: 200
      - label: modified since the epoch, so 412
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${created}"
          otherHeaders:
            - headerName: If-Unmodified-Since
              headerValue: "Thu, 01 Jan 1970 00:00:00 GMT"
        response:
          statusCode: 412
